5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Self-driving cars are pretty cool. They're being hot nowadays, so we're going to use this as an example.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
And in the past, we have seen how we can give an autonomous vehicle a goal,

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,000
some condition we wanted to reach.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
For example, we might have a car that wants to pick up some passengers along the way,

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:44,000
get to some destination, and maybe navigate some world.

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
But let's consider that there's something like a self-driving car.

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,000
We also have a number of other goals or requirements that are sort of implicit along the way,

12
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:59,000
in that we want our self-driving car to obey the rules of the road as it's driving.

13
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
And these aren't just goal conditions, which is a sort of single goal that you're trying to reach,

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
but these are sort of goals that you're trying to maintain throughout the path that you're traveling.

15
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,000
So one example of this is a traffic light.

16
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
So maybe you guys can help me out with this.

17
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Just in plain English, how would you describe the rules of how you would want a car to behave

18
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
when it goes across a traffic light?

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Very simple.

20
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Slow down if you see yellow, stop if you see red, go if you see red.

21
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Yeah, so pretty much, stop if you see red, go if you see green.

22
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,000
And specifically, if you see red and you stop, you're going to want to stop until you see the green,

23
00:01:49,000 --> 00:02:01,000
at which point that sort of condition that you're stopping goes away and you're able to move on.

24
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
In addition to things like opening traffic lights, there might also be some other rules of the road

25
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,000
that we want to follow.

26
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:13,000
For example, we might want to always stay within his speed limit.

27
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
And then there might also be some other practical conditions of driving on the road,

28
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
which is that at some point we're going to need to refuel.

29
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
And if we consider a car that might be driving for a really long time,

30
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
one statement, logical statement we could say about the path that this car takes

31
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
and the sequence of states that it goes through is that at every point in time,

32
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
we want the plan to have some future state when we are going to visit a gas station.

33
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,000
So this isn't just like a single goal that we're trying to reach,

34
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:49,000
but over a very long time, even if we consider that the car could drive in a given amount of time,

35
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,000
at every point in the time, we're going to need to be thinking ahead that there's going to be some time

36
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
when we reach a gas station and reach the border.

37
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:07,000
So these types of conditions, things like staying on a red light until it turns green,

38
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
always staying within a certain speed limit, or always having some path in the future,

39
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
some state in the future when we reach a gas station,

40
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
are things that we can express with a type of logic called temporal logic.

41
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
And there are two things that we hope you're going to be able to do by the time you leave this lecture.

42
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:28,000
The first is to be able to model these temporally extended goals

43
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:34,000
using a specific type of temporal logic called linear temporal logic, or LTL.

44
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:39,000
And secondly, we're going to hope that you'll be able to actually apply this to planning

45
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
great plans with these temporally extended goals.

46
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
And in addition to just sort of this regular planning we've been dealing with,

47
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
actually incorporating this idea of preferences into your plans.

48
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:57,000
So preference basically is not a required condition, but a desired condition

49
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
that you can use to select between alternative paths.

50
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
So you might have many different ways that you could reach your goal,

51
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
but we can use some of these temporally extended goals,

52
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
like maybe you prefer to break as few of the rules of the road as you can,

53
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:21,000
and you can use those as preferences to choose between plans.

54
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:26,000
So just as an outline, first we're just going to do a little introduction to linear temporal logic.

55
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
We're going to talk about what this is, what LTL is, why you want to use it,

56
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,000
and we're going to go through the syntax and the semantics of different LTL operators.

57
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Then we're going to walk you through some example LTL problems

58
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,000
and actually talk about applications to plan,

59
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,000
how you create plans with these linear temporal logic temporal goals.

60
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Finally, we're going to incorporate preferences into this,

61
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
talk about how you express preferences,

62
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
and specifically talk about a language called LPP that allows you to plan

63
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
with temporal logic and preferences.

64
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
So now for the introduction to linear temporal logic.

65
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Temporal logic at its core is a formalism

66
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
because we're just specifying properties of a system that vary with time.

67
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:15,000
So these aren't just conditions that are true at a single state,

68
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,000
which is what we've mostly been dealing with prepositional logic.

69
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:27,000
So we've been saying things like condition A and B but not C are true at a given state,

70
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
and that's prepositional logic.

71
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Temporal logic is sort of a layer on top of that

72
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,000
when we're dealing not just with what's true at a single state,

73
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
but actually extending that over time as the system moves through a sequence of states

74
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
and expressing properties on a temporal level.

75
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,000
So you might have a system that can be represented as a state machine,

76
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:55,000
an automata, and it can go through a sequence of different states.

77
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
And while you might be able to represent the whole system like this,

78
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,000
if we actually execute the system, we're going to get one path through the system.

79
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
That's often called the trace of the system.

80
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
You can also think of it as a timeline of the system.

81
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:13,000
So one example of a timeline of the system is it might start at state A,

82
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:18,000
then go to state C, then go to D, and keep looping around the D forever.

83
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,000
So that's one trace or one timeline of the system,

84
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:25,000
and that's really just a sequence of states that it goes through.

85
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:29,000
And in the past, we've seen this in some of the problem sets, for example,

86
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:35,000
when we were modeling the warp reactor, we've had the valves

87
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
that could transition between open and closed states.

88
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:44,000
And also the whole system of the starship going through different planets,

89
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
transitioning through a larger set of locations, picking up passengers, dropping them off.

90
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:54,000
So these could be quite complex.

91
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:00,000
And so as I said, previously, in our problems that we've been modeling,

92
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,000
we've been going through a system and we've been searching for a single state

93
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
that satisfies all of our cold conditions.

94
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:12,000
Like maybe we want to reach LaVinia and save a certain number of people.

95
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:20,000
But with temporal logic, we can actually express goals that are satisfied over a number of states.

96
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:25,000
And this allows us to do some more expressive goals

97
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:29,000
than we could capture just by looking at a single state at the end.

98
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:38,000
So for example, what if a problem requires some condition to be met

99
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
until another condition is met?

100
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,000
For example, when you see a red light, that implies that we should stop

101
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
until we see the green light.

102
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,000
And if we look at a timeline or a trace of one of these systems,

103
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,000
that might look something like this.

104
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:59,000
So we're going along through our system and at one of the states we see the red light.

105
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,000
So that adds this condition that we should stop.

106
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
And then that condition will hold forever until we see a green light,

107
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:10,000
at which point that condition has dropped and we can keep moving on.

108
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
Another example where we might want to use temporal logic

109
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:18,000
is what if our problem requires a condition to always eventually be met?

110
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
And this is what I was talking about with the gas station.

111
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:26,000
So we might be going on forever and there should always be some point in the future

112
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:30,000
when we do have a state where we're at a gas station.

113
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:34,000
So we might start our system at a certain state

114
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:38,000
and our plan says that at some point we do reach a gas station.

115
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:39,000
So that's good.

116
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:42,000
But then when we get to the next state, we also want it to be true

117
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,000
that from that point on we're going to reach another gas station.

118
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:50,000
And after that, we're going on and on and on into the future.

119
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:56,000
These are things that you can express very easily with the types of logic

120
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:00,000
that we've been dealing with before.

121
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:05,000
So one important distinction that we should clear up before we really dive into the syntax

122
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:11,000
of how these linear temporal logics work is the difference between branching time and linear time.

123
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:15,000
There are really two different types, two different models of time that we can be working with.

124
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:20,000
There's two different types of temporal logic that exist to express these different types of time.

125
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:26,000
So linear time is more similar to the world that we live in.

126
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:30,000
Unless you're living in some sci-fi movie where time is branching into different realities

127
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:36,000
and all sorts of crazy stuff is going on, you can think of time as just this linear timeline.

128
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:40,000
So right now, there's one future state that's going to happen for me.

129
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:47,000
And regardless of how many options I have, there's one realization of the time.

130
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:53,000
And what this happens, what this lets us do is that it gives us a single path for our system.

131
00:09:53,000 --> 00:10:02,000
And we can reason very exactly about the conditions that are met within that path.

132
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:12,000
And this lets us describe what will always happen in a system.

133
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:17,000
For example, we can guarantee that we always stop at a red light until we see a green light,

134
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:23,000
or we always stay within the speed limit.

135
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:28,000
This is contrasted with a different model of time called branching time.

136
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:34,000
And in this model of time, at each time instant, we consider all possible futures.

137
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:39,000
So if we have multiple actions that we can perform at a given state,

138
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:44,000
we're going to consider different timelines that branch off from each of those possible behaviors.

139
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:50,000
And this results in alternate courses of time that we can reason about as a set.

140
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:56,000
And instead of thinking about this as always conditions, so our path always does this,

141
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,000
we can think about this more in possibilities.

142
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,000
And if we're using branching time, we can say, at this state in time,

143
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:07,000
there exists a future path in which we will always stay under the speed limit.

144
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:13,000
Or at this point in time, all of our future paths satisfy a certain condition.

145
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:20,000
So whereas linear temporal logic is more about guaranteeing what will happen on a given path,

146
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:26,000
branching time allows us to reason about what might possibly happen in this system.

147
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:31,000
So for our purposes with planning, in part because it's more practical,

148
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:35,000
and in part because it gives us an exact analysis of a given path,

149
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
we're going to be doing linear time.

150
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:40,000
And for that reason, we use what's called linear temporal logic.

151
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,000
There's a different type of temporal logic.

152
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:47,000
There's one called CTL and CTL star that deal with branching time.

153
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:52,000
And those are sort of an extension of linear temporal logic that add a few other operators

154
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
that allow you to reason over multiple paths.

155
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:00,000
But for our purposes, we're just going to be using the linear time model.

156
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:08,000
So kind of as a recap of what linear temporal logic involves, we're using this linear time model.

157
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:13,000
Another important distinction is that we're actually going to be talking about infinite sequences of states.

158
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:19,000
So instead of us just reasoning up until a certain goal condition is met,

159
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:26,000
we're thinking about systems that could theoretically keep transitioning over time over and over and over into the future.

160
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:31,000
So if you have one of these state machines, you can think at each time step it's going to visit a new state.

161
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:36,000
And if you give it enough time, it's just going to generate an infinite sequence of states.

162
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:40,000
And we can actually express properties that can hold over an infinite number of states,

163
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:46,000
not just the discrete number of states that we might usually think of to reach a goal.

164
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:52,000
And linear temporal logic can actually be modified to work with discrete set of states.

165
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:56,000
It just requires an additional operator.

166
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:02,000
But the ability to reason about infinite sequences of states gives us a lot of power

167
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:09,000
in talking about guarantees for our system regardless of how much time we're talking about.

168
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:17,000
Finally, all the operators we're going to be looking at in our logic system are what we call forward-looking conditions.

169
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:27,000
So when we're given a state, we're going to be reasoning about what's going to happen in the future for potentially infinite amount of time in the future.

170
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:33,000
There's sort of a symmetric version of this logic that reasons about the past.

171
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:40,000
So for example, we could say, talk about guarantees and something will eventually happen in the future.

172
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:48,000
There's sort of a symmetric version where we can talk about, given a certain state, we can guarantee that something eventually happened in the past.

173
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:54,000
But for us, and that we're going to focus on planning, we're typically planning about what to do in the future.

174
00:13:54,000 --> 00:14:01,000
It makes more sense for us to use this future-looking set of operators.

175
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:11,000
And so I guess one little nuance here that I sort of pointed out in the previous slide is that because we're using a linear model of time and not a branching model of time,

176
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:22,000
we're going to be expressing properties that happen over a single path of states and not properties that happen over a separate path of states.

177
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:37,000
So finally, I'm just going to talk about a couple of the main applications for linear temporal logic before Ellie will dive in and actually talk more about the semantics and the syntax of how we use this logic.

178
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:42,000
So the first main use is verification and model checking.

179
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:56,000
We can use this linear temporal logic to create guarantees about a system and formally verify it for a couple of different properties.

180
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,000
For example, we can talk about the safety of the system.

181
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:06,000
We can say over the course of the system, we guarantee that it will never enter a state with a certain condition.

182
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,000
We can also talk about the maintenance of properties.

183
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:18,000
So over the entire lifetime of the system, we guarantee that a certain condition will always hold.

184
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:24,000
The other main application for temporal logic, one that we're going to be focusing on, is for planning.

185
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:28,000
And this is planning using these temporarily extended goals that I've been talking about.

186
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:33,000
And it actually extends nicely to talking about temporarily extended preferences.

187
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:38,000
So this is given that we have a number of different paths that could reach a goal,

188
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:51,000
we can actually look at all of those and see which paths satisfy certain conditions over the sequence of states and use those conditions as a way of choosing which ones we prefer.

189
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:59,000
So with that, Ellie is now going to talk about some of the syntax of linear temporal logic.

190
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:10,000
Hi guys, my name is Ellie and I'm going to talk about the syntax of LTL.

191
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:19,000
So an LTL formula is a series of states and at each state, you can have various propositions that can be either true or false.

192
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:23,000
So for example, is it okay if I erase this?

193
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
Yeah, go ahead.

194
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:39,000
So for example, we can have as Red's animation shows, we have a series of states that goes on to infinity.

195
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:44,000
And at each state, we can have propositions that are either true or false.

196
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:54,000
So the track of like, if you read, could be an example of a proposition, we could be stopped, etc.

197
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:03,000
And there's various logical operators that we can apply to these formulas, these sets of states,

198
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:14,000
and we can determine if these logical operators are either true or false about each set of states, each formula.

199
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:21,000
So there's the not operated, the or operated, the and, the implies, it's an only if, and like I said, the state,

200
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:25,000
each proposition of this week can either be true or false.

201
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:30,000
So just to go into some examples of these.

202
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:40,000
The logical operator true says that there is, essentially what the operator true says is it's not saying, oh, the state is true.

203
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:43,000
The operator just true says that there are states for all infinity.

204
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
So true is always true.

205
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:50,000
So no matter what you had for your states here, the logical operator true holds.

206
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:59,000
However, when you talk about the logical operator true with respect to a certain state or a certain proposition, I mean, then it kind of becomes false.

207
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:08,000
So for example, here, the logical operator red light is true at the first state.

208
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:12,000
If this was green, then the logical operator red would be false at the first state.

209
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
Does that make sense?

210
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
All right.

211
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:25,000
One important thing to note about the logical operators is that they just apply to the very first state in your sequence.

212
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:40,000
So when we're discussing logical operators, we haven't gotten to the point where we can express temporal information about our sequence of states.

213
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
The next operator is not.

214
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:48,000
So as you can see here, the traffic light is red at the first state.

215
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:56,000
So the traffic light is not green holds because the proposition green is not true at the first state.

