00:53:23,000 --> 00:53:28,000
Exactly.

666
00:53:28,000 --> 00:53:34,000
So you have, initially, your first state that you're in, because P has to be true

667
00:53:34,000 --> 00:53:36,000
forever after you get to this first state.

668
00:53:36,000 --> 00:53:38,000
You have an accepting state P.

669
00:53:38,000 --> 00:53:41,000
You can also model not P if you want to.

670
00:53:41,000 --> 00:53:46,000
You can also just have this single state, which would encapsulate exactly what globally is.

671
00:53:46,000 --> 00:53:50,000
So this is kind of how you go from LTL to Lucia-Tomta.

672
00:53:50,000 --> 00:53:53,000
In kind of a real-world scenario, we have multiple LTL formulas.

673
00:53:53,000 --> 00:53:55,000
I'm kind of all combined.

674
00:53:55,000 --> 00:53:58,000
Your Lucia-Tomta are going to look a little bit more complicated than this.

675
00:53:58,000 --> 00:54:01,000
You can have multiple accepting states, like I said.

676
00:54:01,000 --> 00:54:08,000
But it's kind of an escalated model of what an LTL is trying to represent.

677
00:54:08,000 --> 00:54:10,000
So this is a guesser.

678
00:54:10,000 --> 00:54:11,000
Right.

679
00:54:11,000 --> 00:54:13,000
We have the not P transition out of there.

680
00:54:13,000 --> 00:54:17,000
Like you could possibly come into S0, you would accept Lucia-Tomta,

681
00:54:17,000 --> 00:54:19,000
but then you would do it in S1, right?

682
00:54:19,000 --> 00:54:22,000
So accepting a Lucia-Tomta is a little confusing.

683
00:54:22,000 --> 00:54:27,000
A Lucia-Tomta is only valid if the infinite sequence of states satisfies it.

684
00:54:27,000 --> 00:54:31,000
So if I were to go to P, then I would do a not P.

685
00:54:31,000 --> 00:54:36,000
And my sequence of transitions with P, P, P, not P,

686
00:54:36,000 --> 00:54:41,000
then that would not be a valid sequence of transitions from this Lucia-Tomta,

687
00:54:41,000 --> 00:54:43,000
because I'm transitioning out of my accepting state,

688
00:54:43,000 --> 00:54:46,000
which I need to have been in for an infinite amount of time.

689
00:54:46,000 --> 00:54:49,000
And there's no way I can get back to this.

690
00:54:55,000 --> 00:54:58,000
So there's also a more defined algorithm.

691
00:54:58,000 --> 00:55:01,000
I'm just looking at two of the ways to build a Lucia-Tomta.

692
00:55:01,000 --> 00:55:02,000
There's an algorithm.

693
00:55:02,000 --> 00:55:05,000
The one that's most popularly used was developed by GERF.

694
00:55:05,000 --> 00:55:09,000
This is kind of a Sudoku version of the algorithm.

695
00:55:09,000 --> 00:55:11,000
You go from LTL to Lucia.

696
00:55:11,000 --> 00:55:13,000
As you notice, it actually uses progression,

697
00:55:13,000 --> 00:55:19,000
which is something that planners that take PDDL use to generate plans.

698
00:55:19,000 --> 00:55:23,000
An important thing to note, which I don't want to too much detail,

699
00:55:23,000 --> 00:55:26,000
this algorithm generates a generalized Lucia-Tomta,

700
00:55:26,000 --> 00:55:30,000
which you then need to change to a simple Lucia-Tomta.

701
00:55:30,000 --> 00:55:35,000
The difference is a generalized Lucia-Tomta has a set of sets of accepting states.

702
00:55:35,000 --> 00:55:38,000
So for a generalized Lucia-Tomta to be accepted,

703
00:55:38,000 --> 00:55:43,000
you need to visit an accepting state in each one of those sets of accepting states.

704
00:55:43,000 --> 00:55:46,000
At least one of those states has to be listed.

705
00:55:46,000 --> 00:55:50,000
For a simple Lucia-Tomta, you only have one set of accepting states,

706
00:55:50,000 --> 00:55:54,000
and you can visit any one of those states for the Lucia-Tomta to be valid.

707
00:55:54,000 --> 00:55:57,000
The translation between those two is a little bit more complicated,

708
00:55:57,000 --> 00:56:01,000
and we'll have papers in our reference that will walk through how to do that.

709
00:56:04,000 --> 00:56:07,000
This is the progression algorithm.

710
00:56:07,000 --> 00:56:12,000
Basically, it just tells you how if you have an LTO formula F,

711
00:56:12,000 --> 00:56:17,000
and some current state N, and some usually they involve some time step,

712
00:56:17,000 --> 00:56:19,000
because we're in the real world and we can't really model

713
00:56:19,000 --> 00:56:23,000
in an amount of time and we have to make it discrete.

714
00:56:23,000 --> 00:56:27,000
So if you have some time step between successive states,

715
00:56:27,000 --> 00:56:33,000
how do you push certain LTO formulas onto next states to be evaluated later?

716
00:56:33,000 --> 00:56:37,000
For example, I'll take this next F as an example.

717
00:56:37,000 --> 00:56:41,000
So if F was the next F of some LTO formula,

718
00:56:41,000 --> 00:56:45,000
you would need to append that formula to the next state

719
00:56:45,000 --> 00:56:49,000
to be evaluated in the next state to see if that's going to be true or not.

720
00:56:49,000 --> 00:56:55,000
Some are things for an until, and we'll go through this formally,

721
00:56:55,000 --> 00:56:58,000
but it's here for reference.

722
00:56:58,000 --> 00:57:02,000
So the next step to this process is going from Lucia-Tomta to PDDL2.

723
00:57:03,000 --> 00:57:06,000
This process is confusing for me to understand.

724
00:57:06,000 --> 00:57:11,000
So the first thing I'll say is that Lucia states are not equivalent to traditional PDDL states.

725
00:57:11,000 --> 00:57:17,000
In a PDDL state, if you have two states that have the same propositions that are true or false,

726
00:57:17,000 --> 00:57:19,000
those states are identical.

727
00:57:19,000 --> 00:57:22,000
In Lucia-Tomta, that's not necessarily true.

728
00:57:22,000 --> 00:57:28,000
In Lucia-Tomta, you also have to encapsulate which transitions you can make out of each state.

729
00:57:28,000 --> 00:57:35,000
So F1 and F2 could have the same set of propositions that are true and that hold,

730
00:57:35,000 --> 00:57:39,000
but out of F1, I should put that back up.

731
00:57:39,000 --> 00:57:44,000
This is the Lucia-Tomta for future globally, which is what Professor Williams is talking about.

732
00:57:44,000 --> 00:57:51,000
So if I enter in F1, the start means I have any sort of transitions that I want on F1,

733
00:57:51,000 --> 00:57:54,000
and then at some point I need the transition P,

734
00:57:54,000 --> 00:57:58,000
and when I'm in S2, I'm in P for I had to take transition P forever.

735
00:58:00,000 --> 00:58:09,000
However, if I get a sequence of inputs, let's say I get P and then B, and then P again,

736
00:58:09,000 --> 00:58:12,000
and then P again, it's not clear if I'm in S1 or S2,

737
00:58:12,000 --> 00:58:15,000
because in S1, I can execute any kind of state I want,

738
00:58:15,000 --> 00:58:21,000
and in S1, I can execute any transition I want, and in S1, 2, I have to execute only P.

739
00:58:21,000 --> 00:58:25,000
So if I got a sequence of P's, I can be in either state.

740
00:58:25,000 --> 00:58:30,000
So you need something else to act Pdl2 to basically determine which state you're in

741
00:58:30,000 --> 00:58:33,000
and which transitions you can make from that state.

742
00:58:39,000 --> 00:58:47,000
So there's two ways you can kind of transform Pdl2 to kind of encapsulate what Lucia-Tomta encapsulates.

743
00:58:47,000 --> 00:58:53,000
The first thing is you can create new actions that encapsulate the allowable transitions in each state.

744
00:58:53,000 --> 00:58:57,000
This would basically mean that for every action you have in your traditional Pdl,

745
00:58:57,000 --> 00:59:02,000
you have to create an action for basically repetitive actions for each one of these states,

746
00:59:02,000 --> 00:59:06,000
so you know exactly which transition you can take out of that state.

747
00:59:06,000 --> 00:59:11,000
This could get long because you have to make a new action for every single one of these states

748
00:59:11,000 --> 00:59:15,000
for every action that you want to take, but that is one way you can do it.

749
00:59:16,000 --> 00:59:19,000
Another way you can do it is introducing derived predicates.

750
00:59:19,000 --> 00:59:22,000
Derived predicates are predicates that don't depend on the actions at all

751
00:59:22,000 --> 00:59:25,000
and just depend on some other formula in your plan.

752
00:59:25,000 --> 00:59:30,000
So for example, we have a derived predicate that explicitly tells you whether you're in S1

753
00:59:30,000 --> 00:59:35,000
or whether you're in S2, and you can use these predicates in your actions in Pdl2

754
00:59:35,000 --> 00:59:40,000
to make sure you know which state S1 or S2 you're in and you're in Lucia-Tomta.

755
00:59:40,000 --> 00:59:46,000
And then in your formulation of your plan, you set your final state to be one of the accepted states.

756
00:59:46,000 --> 00:59:52,000
So I guess that kind of answers one of the questions about how you go from infinite to finite.

757
00:59:52,000 --> 00:59:56,000
The accepting state is usually when you place as your final state.

758
00:59:56,000 --> 01:00:00,000
And then the derived predicates appear purely in the preconditions?

759
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:01,000
Or do they also appear in the effects?

760
01:00:01,000 --> 01:00:03,000
They also appear in the effects.

761
01:00:03,000 --> 01:00:08,000
Because you need to know if you have to envision out of S1 to S2.

762
01:00:08,000 --> 01:00:13,000
So you likely have a derived predicate for both S1 and a derived predicate for S2.

763
01:00:13,000 --> 01:00:17,000
And that is also kind of a lot of work to add to your Pdl2 problem,

764
01:00:17,000 --> 01:00:19,000
but it's definitely shorting them the first way.

765
01:00:19,000 --> 01:00:23,000
And it definitely encapsulates everything that Lucia-Tomta does.

766
01:00:23,000 --> 01:00:26,000
Does anyone have any questions about that?

767
01:00:26,000 --> 01:00:28,000
So you are changing the action then?

768
01:00:28,000 --> 01:00:34,000
Yeah, so you're changing the action, but you don't need to make a new action for every state.

769
01:00:34,000 --> 01:00:41,000
In the first formulation, let's say I have some traditional action, like move block or something.

770
01:00:41,000 --> 01:00:44,000
I have to create a move block action for each one of these states,

771
01:00:44,000 --> 01:00:48,000
because I need to know exactly which transitions I can take out of that action.

772
01:00:48,000 --> 01:00:49,000
And the second example?

773
01:00:49,000 --> 01:00:52,000
And then the second one, I can just have one move block,

774
01:00:52,000 --> 01:00:58,000
but in my move block I'll have a derived predicate that tells me which state I'm in,

775
01:00:58,000 --> 01:01:03,000
which state I'm in when I execute that action.

776
01:01:03,000 --> 01:01:08,000
So the number of actions you have to write is less,

777
01:01:08,000 --> 01:01:15,000
because you explicitly have predicates that are telling you which state that you're in at each time.

778
01:01:15,000 --> 01:01:18,000
Now I'm going to hand it over to Mark if there are no more questions.

779
01:01:18,000 --> 01:01:23,000
I'm going to hand it over to Mark, and I'm just going to talk about predicates.

780
01:01:23,000 --> 01:01:27,000
Alright, so we're getting a little low on time.

781
01:01:27,000 --> 01:01:28,000
That's okay.

782
01:01:28,000 --> 01:01:31,000
So now I'll transition to talking about, we've talked about template standard goals,

783
01:01:31,000 --> 01:01:34,000
and we've said that goals are things that always have to be true.

784
01:01:34,000 --> 01:01:36,000
So now let's talk about preferences,

785
01:01:36,000 --> 01:01:38,000
preferences are things that don't necessarily have to be true,

786
01:01:38,000 --> 01:01:41,000
but the things that you'd like to be true.

787
01:01:41,000 --> 01:01:45,000
So in the classical planning problem, we can formulate it like that.

788
01:01:45,000 --> 01:01:47,000
We have a set of states, S, initial state S0,

789
01:01:47,000 --> 01:01:50,000
a set of operators, and a set of goal states.

790
01:01:50,000 --> 01:01:57,000
And the problem is to transition from the initial state to any state that fulfills your goal state using the operators in the LAA.

791
01:01:57,000 --> 01:01:59,000
In a preference-based planning problem,

792
01:01:59,000 --> 01:02:02,000
we introduce this additional field R,

793
01:02:02,000 --> 01:02:07,000
which is a partial or total relation expressing preferences between plans.

794
01:02:07,000 --> 01:02:10,000
This is a little preference symbol right there.

795
01:02:10,000 --> 01:02:14,000
So that basically tells you if there are multiple plans to accomplish your goal,

796
01:02:14,000 --> 01:02:17,000
which one do you want to use?

797
01:02:17,000 --> 01:02:23,000
And again, preferences are properties that are desired but not necessarily required.

798
01:02:23,000 --> 01:02:27,000
So there are two kind of different types of preference languages,

799
01:02:27,000 --> 01:02:29,000
quantitative and qualitative.

800
01:02:29,000 --> 01:02:31,000
As you would expect in quantitative languages,

801
01:02:31,000 --> 01:02:36,000
we actually assign an American value to the different plans in order to compare them.

802
01:02:36,000 --> 01:02:39,000
So this plan is a weight of four and a weight of two,

803
01:02:39,000 --> 01:02:41,000
and that tells you which one you prefer.

804
01:02:41,000 --> 01:02:43,000
Here are some languages that do that.

805
01:02:43,000 --> 01:02:47,000
For qualitative languages, we actually just have this type of plan.

806
01:02:47,000 --> 01:02:51,000
This plan with this property is preferred to this other plan with this property.

807
01:02:51,000 --> 01:02:57,000
But we don't actually know any sort of information about how much we prefer one to the other.

808
01:02:57,000 --> 01:03:01,000
An important outcome of this is when you have quantitative languages,

809
01:03:01,000 --> 01:03:02,000
you're totally comparable.

810
01:03:02,000 --> 01:03:05,000
So if any two sets of plans, you can determine which one is preferred.

811
01:03:05,000 --> 01:03:10,000
Whereas in qualitative languages, you could have a situation where plan one is preferred to plan two,

812
01:03:10,000 --> 01:03:12,000
and plan one is also preferred to plan three,

813
01:03:12,000 --> 01:03:16,000
but that doesn't give you any information about whether plan two is preferred to plan.

814
01:03:16,000 --> 01:03:19,000
So it's a little bit less than the rest of them.

815
01:03:21,000 --> 01:03:25,000
So to go into how you actually express preferences in PGDL3,

816
01:03:25,000 --> 01:03:27,000
like you talked about with temporally extended goals,

817
01:03:27,000 --> 01:03:30,000
there is some PGDL3 syntax to do this.

818
01:03:30,000 --> 01:03:33,000
There's a label, the preference label here,

819
01:03:33,000 --> 01:03:37,000
which you can put on fluence to represent things that you prefer.

820
01:03:37,000 --> 01:03:39,000
And then there's a function called is violated

821
01:03:39,000 --> 01:03:41,000
that essentially returns a number of times

822
01:03:41,000 --> 01:03:46,000
that any fluent that has a preference label was not satisfied in your plan.

823
01:03:46,000 --> 01:03:48,000
For example, if you had the preference,

824
01:03:48,000 --> 01:03:50,000
you could have the latest green until it turns red.

825
01:03:50,000 --> 01:03:55,000
So here's our PGDL3 temporally extended, in this case preference language,

826
01:03:55,000 --> 01:03:57,000
and we label it with the preference label here.

827
01:03:57,000 --> 01:03:59,000
This is just a name.

828
01:03:59,000 --> 01:04:03,000
And then our metric, our plan basically tries to minimize the number of times

829
01:04:03,000 --> 01:04:05,000
that this preference is violated.

830
01:04:05,000 --> 01:04:11,000
So that's one way to actually express preferences directly in PGDL3.

831
01:04:11,000 --> 01:04:14,000
All right, so now I'm going to talk about LDP.

832
01:04:14,000 --> 01:04:17,000
LDP is a different language that is quite expressive

833
01:04:17,000 --> 01:04:20,000
in terms of the types of preferences you can represent.

834
01:04:20,000 --> 01:04:22,000
It's quantable languages, which the language,

835
01:04:22,000 --> 01:04:24,000
which means we're going to be expressing,

836
01:04:24,000 --> 01:04:27,000
we're going to have weights for each of our plans to express preferences,

837
01:04:27,000 --> 01:04:30,000
and we can actually express kind of the strength of the preference label.

838
01:04:30,000 --> 01:04:34,000
It is preferred 20 to 30 times as much as it will be.

839
01:04:34,000 --> 01:04:36,000
It's the extension of the old language named PGDL3,

840
01:04:36,000 --> 01:04:41,000
and here's the bigger if you want to actually check out this detail.

841
01:04:41,000 --> 01:04:43,000
The formulas are constructed hierarchically,

842
01:04:43,000 --> 01:04:47,000
so I'll go through briefly how we actually construct these preference formulas.

843
01:04:47,000 --> 01:04:51,000
So the lowest level is called a basic desired formula for BDL,

844
01:04:51,000 --> 01:04:54,000
and that just expresses our temporally extended proposition.

845
01:04:54,000 --> 01:04:56,000
So this is just straight up LT on this,

846
01:04:56,000 --> 01:04:58,000
because we saw it in the first section of the presentation.

847
01:04:58,000 --> 01:05:02,000
So I'm going to use kind of a cooking dinner example through these slides.

848
01:05:02,000 --> 01:05:05,000
So in this case, we always just use future operators.

849
01:05:05,000 --> 01:05:07,000
So at some point I might want to cook as part of our plan,

850
01:05:07,000 --> 01:05:10,000
maybe I want to order takeout to eat dinner,

851
01:05:10,000 --> 01:05:12,000
and then I have some, you know, eating spaghetti or eating pizza.

852
01:05:12,000 --> 01:05:15,000
Those are two different things, two different options that I could have in the plan.

853
01:05:15,000 --> 01:05:20,000
I don't have to have either of those, but it was just obvious that I could have.

854
01:05:20,000 --> 01:05:21,000
So that's the lowest level.

855
01:05:21,000 --> 01:05:24,000
The next level is called atomic preference formulas, or APFs.

856
01:05:24,000 --> 01:05:29,000
These are where we express our preferences between the BDFs that we informed in the previous slide.

857
01:05:29,000 --> 01:05:31,000
So in this example, I'm using weight,

858
01:05:31,000 --> 01:05:35,000
specifically to represent preferences for lower weights being preferred.

859
01:05:35,000 --> 01:05:39,000
So here we're saying I prefer to cook over ordering takeouts.

860
01:05:39,000 --> 01:05:43,000
So this is the BDF for cooking, this is the BDF for ordering takeout,

861
01:05:43,000 --> 01:05:47,000
and we can apply weights to those to express which one is preferred

862
01:05:47,000 --> 01:05:49,000
and how much is preferred over the previous slide.

863
01:05:49,000 --> 01:05:54,000
And here's the preferred amount of spaghetti over eating pizza.

864
01:05:54,000 --> 01:05:56,000
So that's the second level.

865
01:05:56,000 --> 01:05:59,000
The third level is called general preference formulas, or GPFs.

866
01:05:59,000 --> 01:06:02,000
And these are where we can do conjunctions or disjunctions

867
01:06:02,000 --> 01:06:04,000
or qualification of our previous formats.

868
01:06:04,000 --> 01:06:09,000
So for example, if we want to say, in our previous slide, we had two APFs.

869
01:06:09,000 --> 01:06:13,000
We preferred to cook and we preferred to eat spaghetti.

870
01:06:13,000 --> 01:06:19,000
And here we can express that we really don't care which one of these we want to satisfy.

871
01:06:19,000 --> 01:06:23,000
You can think of this as we basically try to satisfy the APF with the lowest weight.

872
01:06:23,000 --> 01:06:25,000
So if we have this OR operating here,

873
01:06:25,000 --> 01:06:28,000
that means that our planner is going to try and satisfy the lowest weight

874
01:06:28,000 --> 01:06:31,000
among all these different options here, which in this case is referring to cook.

875
01:06:31,000 --> 01:06:33,000
That's its first goal.

876
01:06:33,000 --> 01:06:36,000
You can also use an AND operator, for example,

877
01:06:36,000 --> 01:06:40,000
and that's going to minimize the maximum weight across both of those options.

878
01:06:40,000 --> 01:06:48,000
So basically what it's going to try and do is minimize the highest weight among these two options.

879
01:06:48,000 --> 01:06:52,000
So it's going to try and order, sorry, it's going to try and cook,

880
01:06:52,000 --> 01:06:57,000
and it's going to try and eat spaghetti versus doing these other options.

881
01:06:57,000 --> 01:06:59,000
Okay, so those are GPFs.

882
01:06:59,000 --> 01:07:04,000
And now I'm moving on to activated preferences, which are the highest level.

883
01:07:04,000 --> 01:07:09,000
So with APFs, these define the order in which our different preferences are waxed.

884
01:07:09,000 --> 01:07:12,000
You can, of course, express a lot of different preferences for your planner,

885
01:07:12,000 --> 01:07:14,000
but not all of them may be achievable,

886
01:07:14,000 --> 01:07:17,000
especially if you're trying to achieve a large number of preferences at the same time.

887
01:07:17,000 --> 01:07:20,000
You may not actually be able to achieve all of those.

888
01:07:20,000 --> 01:07:25,000
So how do we reduce our set in the correct order or in the preferred order

889
01:07:25,000 --> 01:07:28,000
so that our planner to end up with isn't even the most preferred planner?

890
01:07:28,000 --> 01:07:30,000
That's what APFs allow you to do.

891
01:07:30,000 --> 01:07:35,000
